  "key_judgments": [
    {
      "issue": "Initial admission estimand",
      "judgment": "Initial service decision is A(S,C), the same for all24 evaluation futures under one fit. Per-original-path admission frequency describes the five fitted training sets that held that path out; it does not mean the controller sees the unknown future or admits customer subpopulations by future risk."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Zero-auto repeat",
      "judgment": "Repeat1 rejects Q2 execution for all120 validation identities under both rules. Conditional risk and performance are NULL, not zero. This is algorithm abstention under the declared empirical rules, not missing computation."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Risk reference stability",
      "judgment": "Primary repeat2 has8/48=16.67% observed full-H events and canonical has5/24=20.83%. Aggregate primary repeat3 equals12/120=10%; this equality is no guarantee, and no single favorable repeat may be chosen."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Sample size and causality",
      "judgment": "Every outer training sample is96 and dynamic inner sample72. Different outer memberships, inner partitions, gamma, common targets, fans and resulting states vary together. Cross-fit changes cannot be uniquely attributed to sample-size rounding, liquidity or a specific forecast omission."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Parameter versus decision stability",
      "judgment": "Different beta/eta frequencies do not prove different actions, unique preferences or statistical non-identifiability. Here the primary and canonical methods have identical observed financial outcomes on their common admitted168 records; the effect is additional initial admission, not demonstrated better common-path trading."
    },
    {
      "issue": "Risk/coverage interpretation",
      "judgment": "The primary admits more fitted runs, but the added repeated group has14 events among120 observed records. These are dependent scenario-fit outcomes. Do not report14/120 as an external risk estimate or call either method safer solely from unequal conditional fractions."
    },
    {
      "issue": "No outcome-driven repair",
      "judgment": "Do not select a favorable seed/repeat or switch to canonical because of these outcomes. Preserve the fixed primary as an experimental candidate and separately freeze any future change to risk evidence, fan or policy family."
    }
  ],
